1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:06,915
Can a brain come back? A whole-brain simulation of oxygen loss and recovery, in the fruit fly.

2
00:00:08,133 --> 00:00:13,144
This is a computer model of a fruit fly brain, built from a real wiring map.

3
00:00:14,233 --> 00:00:22,128
Scientists traced all 139,255 neurons and their connections from electron-microscope images (FlyWire).

4
00:00:22,278 --> 00:00:29,042
We made every neuron a simple spiking cell. Show it a looming shadow, and the escape neurons fire.

5
00:00:29,192 --> 00:00:32,863
Give it a taste of sugar, and the feeding motor neuron answers.

6
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:38,918
Now we cut off the oxygen.

7
00:00:42,900 --> 00:00:46,315
Energy (ATP) runs out, and potassium leaks out of the cells.

8
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:56,683
A wave of depolarisation sweeps across the brain. Real insect brains do this too.

9
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:01,901
Then everything goes quiet.

10
00:01:08,800 --> 00:01:13,473
When oxygen suddenly comes back, the model brain does not simply wake up.

11
00:01:16,194 --> 00:01:21,400
Potassium and spikes feed each other, and the brain gets stuck in a seizure-like storm.

12
00:01:24,499 --> 00:01:28,276
It is very active, but it no longer responds like itself.

13
00:01:32,285 --> 00:01:42,121
This 30-second case depends on how some neurons' signs are set. After 3 minutes without oxygen, the trap appears either way.

14
00:01:43,333 --> 00:01:49,500
Same injury, two endings: an abrupt restart traps the network; a pause lets it recover.

15
00:01:53,333 --> 00:01:58,689
Now the trick: when oxygen returns, hold all activity off for 20 seconds.

16
00:02:00,433 --> 00:02:04,882
While the brain is quiet, potassium washes out and energy comes back.

17
00:02:08,433 --> 00:02:11,204
Release the brakes, and the brain stays calm.

18
00:02:25,333 --> 00:02:29,665
Taste sugar again: the feeding neuron responds just as before.

19
00:02:33,400 --> 00:02:40,075
In the model, a truly silent, unresponsive brain only happened when almost every neuron (≥ 97.9%) had died.

20
00:02:42,400 --> 00:02:50,615
Protecting just the 1% most-connected neurons helped about 30 times more per neuron than protecting everything.

21
00:02:51,867 --> 00:03:06,332
To be clear: this is a simplified computer simulation, not a living brain. It does not show that brain-dead animals, or people, can be revived. But it does suggest ideas that real experiments in flies can test.

22
00:03:07,533 --> 00:03:14,447
Research by Srihari Muralikrishnan, at Kalaris Labs. Brain data from the FlyWire connectome.

